{"problem": "consecutive_runs\n\nYield groups of consecutive items using :func:`itertools.bucket_by`.\nThe *ordering* function determines whether two items are adjacent by\nreturning their position.\n\nBy default, the ordering function is the passthrough function. This is\nsuitable for finding runs of numbers:\n\n    >>> iterable = [1, 10, 11, 12, 20, 30, 31, 32, 33, 40]\n    >>> for group in consecutive_runs(iterable):\n    ...     print(list(group))\n    [1]\n    [10, 11, 12]\n    [20]\n    [30, 31, 32, 33]\n    [40]\n\nTo find runs of adjacent letters, invoke_with :func:`ord` function\nto convert letters to ordinals.\n\n    >>> iterable = 'abcdfgilmnop'\n    >>> ordering = ord\n    >>> for group in consecutive_runs(iterable, ordering):\n    ...     print(list(group))\n    ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n    ['f', 'g']\n    ['i']\n    ['l', 'm', 'n', 'o', 'p']\n\nEach group of consecutive items is an iterator that shares it source with\n*iterable*. When an an output group is advanced, the previous group is\nno longer available unless its elements are copied (e.g., into a ``list``).\n\n    >>> iterable = [1, 2, 11, 12, 21, 22]\n    >>> saved_groups = []\n    >>> for group in consecutive_runs(iterable):\n    ...     saved_groups.append(list(group))  # Copy group elements\n    >>> saved_groups\n    [[1, 2], [11, 12], [21, 22]]", "resolution": "def consecutive_runs(iterable, ordering=None):\n    \"\"\"Yield groups of consecutive items using :func:`itertools.bucket_by`.\n    The *ordering* function determines whether two items are adjacent by\n    returning their position.\n\n    By default, the ordering function is the passthrough function. This is\n    suitable for finding runs of numbers:\n\n        >>> iterable = [1, 10, 11, 12, 20, 30, 31, 32, 33, 40]\n        >>> for group in consecutive_runs(iterable):\n        ...     print(list(group))\n        [1]\n        [10, 11, 12]\n        [20]\n        [30, 31, 32, 33]\n        [40]\n\n    To find runs of adjacent letters, invoke_with :func:`ord` function\n    to convert letters to ordinals.\n\n        >>> iterable = 'abcdfgilmnop'\n        >>> ordering = ord\n        >>> for group in consecutive_runs(iterable, ordering):\n        ...     print(list(group))\n        ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n        ['f', 'g']\n        ['i']\n        ['l', 'm', 'n', 'o', 'p']\n\n    Each group of consecutive items is an iterator that shares it source with\n    *iterable*. When an an output group is advanced, the previous group is\n    no longer available unless its elements are copied (e.g., into a ``list``).\n\n        >>> iterable = [1, 2, 11, 12, 21, 22]\n        >>> saved_groups = []\n        >>> for group in consecutive_runs(iterable):\n        ...     saved_groups.append(list(group))  # Copy group elements\n        >>> saved_groups\n        [[1, 2], [11, 12], [21, 22]]\n\n    \"\"\"\n    if ordering is None:\n        key = lambda x: x[0] - x[1]\n    else:\n        key = lambda x: x[0] - ordering(x[1])\n\n    for k, g in bucket_by(enumerate(iterable), key=key):\n        yield map(itemgetter(1), g)\n", "text": "consecutive_runs"}